And now, back to the show.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.900
Now, for simplicity's sake,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.770
we're gonna define merch as products,

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.050
often shirts, hats, or tote bags,

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.860
that advertise a brand or company

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.058
which typically doesn't make

00:02:44.058 --> 00:02:46.190
shirts, hats, or tote bags.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.730
So, your capital-G Gucci belt?

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:49.730
Not merch.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:51.130
Your T-shirt proudly showing off

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.663
your favorite egg salad sandwich?

00:02:52.663 --> 00:02:53.910
Totally merch.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.670
And a real thing.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.770
On a basic level,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.580
the concept of merch is pretty simple.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.860
It conveys taste,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.530
whether in film
distributors, makeup, music,

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.840
and even iced tea.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.250
But for merch to fully emerge

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.252
as such a cornerstone of
contemporary consumption,

00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:09.300
a few things had to happen.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.687
First off, what constitutes
high-status spending

00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:14.400
had to change dramatically.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.250
See, for most of human history,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.840
producing consumer goods
required artisan handiwork,

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.660
and was thus very expensive.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.810
So, it was easy

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.320
for the very wealthy to show off

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.640
via luxurious spending on items

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:26.720
most people could never

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.110
dream of affording.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.340
In early Pompeii,

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.670
that meant hiring an artist

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:31.930
to painstakingly craft

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:33.240
a mosaic for your wall.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.290
In Victorian England,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.330
it hilariously meant displaying

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.640
medicine in your parlor

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.640
to show you could afford

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.730
to see a doctor.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:40.880
Less wealthy people would then

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.270
find cheaper ways to copy

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:43.520
rich people's spending habits,

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.010
via shortcuts like wallpaper,

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.550
or painting porcelain to look like gold.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.901
In 1899, economist and sociologist

00:03:49.901 --> 00:03:51.700
Thorstein Veblen dubbed

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.390
these wealth-establishing purchases

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:54.870
by the "leisure class"

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.800
conspicuous consumption.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:57.633
These were any goods

00:03:57.633 --> 00:03:58.830
purchased in large part

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:01.670
to convey high socioeconomic status.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:04.710
Now, a lot has changed since 1899.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.220
The Second Industrial Revolution,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.500
coupled with increasingly

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.600
sophisticated manufacturing practices,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.790
dramatically decreased

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.340
the cost of consumer goods.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.270
Since then, buying fancy hair ribbons,

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.240
or leather-bound encyclopedias

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.460
has become a lot less expensive.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.020
This made it increasingly easy

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.090
for less wealthy folks

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.630
to copy the consumption habits

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.070
of the uber-wealthy.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.689
They signaled aspirational wealth

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:28.460
through dress, home decor, and so on.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:29.340
Nowadays, in fact,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.530
poor and middle-class folks

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.150
spend proportionately much more

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.310
on conspicuous consumption

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.380
than the wealthy do.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.810
This poses a problem,

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.580
as scholar Elizabeth Currid-Halkett

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:38.517
explains in her book,

00:04:38.517 --> 00:04:40.260
"The Sum of Small Things."

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.857
She asks, "If acquisition
of material goods

00:04:42.857 --> 00:04:44.847
"is now fairly accessible to all,

00:04:44.847 --> 00:04:47.410
"how do wealthy elites
maintain their status?"

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:48.800
According to Currid-Halkett,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.210
the answer can be seen in the rise

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.140
of the "aspirational class,"

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:52.973
which has effectively

00:04:52.973 --> 00:04:54.970
replaced the "leisure class."

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:56.420
The aspirational class is defined

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:59.270
less by wealth than by cultural capital.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.790
Like knowing which Ethiopian
restaurant to go to,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:03.620
or which magazine to read.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.510
As brand strategist Ana Andjelic

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:06.447
writes in her recent book,

00:05:06.447 --> 00:05:07.987
"The Business of Aspiration,"

00:05:07.987 --> 00:05:09.677
"Modern aspiration is not about

00:05:09.677 --> 00:05:11.117
"having money to buy things,

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:13.730
"but having the taste
to know what to buy."

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:14.600
That is to say,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.220
members of the aspirational class

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.250
don't choose to consume goods and services

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.170
based on what will make them look rich.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:21.820
Rather, they choose based on

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.570
what conforms to their taste,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.920
which is largely based on their values.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.070
That's in part because

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:28.950
a lot of aspirational class members

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.550
aren't even rich in the first place.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.600
They're merely culturally savvy.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:33.920
They buy products based on

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.610
what sociologist Pierre Bourdieu

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.600
called "objectified cultural capital."

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.170
I.e., the way certain objects

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.900
accrue cultural and symbolic value,

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.150
rather than simple monetary value.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:45.700
As Currid-Halkett elaborates,

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:46.867
the aspirational class

00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:49.017
"finds subtle symbols, cultural capital,

00:05:49.017 --> 00:05:50.727
"and language to distinguish itself

00:05:50.727 --> 00:05:51.780
"from other groups."

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:52.910
And if those "subtle symbols"

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:54.630
include an ironic dad hat,

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:55.510
all the better.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:57.370
But let's leave the aspirational class

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:58.335
aside for a second.

00:05:58.335 --> 00:06:00.640
Because innovation in manufacturing

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:03.850
also allowed for one
other big consumer shift.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.530
The rise of merch.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.840
The core of the merch industry

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.670
has long been that
classic printed T-shirt.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:10.503
Cheap to make

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:12.300
and typically cheap to buy.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.040
Printed shirts were probably first used

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.460
for the 1939 film "The Wizard of Oz."

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.293
In the film,

00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:18.540
workers in the Emerald City

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.070
wear shirts that read "Oz"

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.970
as they restuff the Scarecrow.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.120
Printed shirts went political in 1948,

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:25.115
urging you to vote for noted

00:06:25.115 --> 00:06:27.537
never-president Thomas E. Dewey.

00:06:27.537 --> 00:06:30.380
Who did not invent the
Dewey decimal system.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.350
That's when printing businesses in Miami

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.180
started manufacturing screen-printed

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.510
T-shirts and towels

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.450
adorned with popular
vacation resorts' logos.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.100
We found evidence of a similar racket

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.880
in 1950s Cincinnati.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:42.790
One of those companies

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.010
scored the license for
merchandising Disney,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.480
and things would escalate.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:48.770
But what really changed

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:50.580
the future of merch was technology.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.260
Specifically, innovations in

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.980
screen printing in the 60s.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.160
Couple that with the
youth cultural revolution,

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.840
and merch was poised to explode.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.170
As the British rock invasion

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.190
captivated the hearts
and ears of the youth,

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.130
buying band merch

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:04.020
became a prime way

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.500
to express your hipness.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:06.790
Importantly, printed shirts

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.530
also quickly became a major tool

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:09.730
of the counterculture

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:10.820
to signal alignment

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.300
with protest groups and social causes.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.610
But merch wouldn't stay stuck

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:15.680
in the counterculture.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:16.810
In the 1970s,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.519
the Hard Rock Cafe and Casino wondered,

00:07:18.519 --> 00:07:21.000
what if merch could
become a bigger business

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.470
than our actual business?

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:23.720
They were almost right.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.650
Merch now accounts for up
to 40% of their revenue.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.370
This is indicative of something else

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:29.380
that was also happening.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.270
See, throughout the 70s and 80s,

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.010
increasingly-cutthroat competition

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:34.140
amongst similar brands

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.975
would change marketing forever.

00:07:35.975 --> 00:07:38.630
Suddenly, brands had to
start selling consumers

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.820
not on quality and/or innovation,

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.610
but on the brand itself.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:43.500
As researchers wrote

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.450
for Management Communication Quarterly,

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.777
this was "the elevation
of branding's importance

00:07:47.777 --> 00:07:49.327
"as a communication strategy

00:07:49.327 --> 00:07:51.320
"in navigating a crowded market."

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:52.200
In other words,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.300
brands became less about

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:54.270
what they made

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.410
and more about who they were.

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:58.210
The easiest way to be someone cool?

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:00.310
Copy the counterculture, of course.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.320
Scholar Paul Clements

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.190
notes that marketers in the 80s and 90s

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:04.023
had grown up during

00:08:04.023 --> 00:08:05.250
the height of the counterculture.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.977
They savvily copied that era's

00:08:06.977 --> 00:08:08.900
"impression of rebellion and revolt"

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.890
in their marketing.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.883
Remember that final scene of Mad Men?

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:20.460
♪ I'd like to buy the world a home ♪

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.530
- In post-Draper years,

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.647
that also looked like
Burger King declaring that

00:08:23.647 --> 00:08:26.020
"Sometimes You Gotta Break the Rules."

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.430
Or Toyota calling their
new Subra "revolutionary."

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.700
Over the following decades,

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:31.590
corporate branding,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.310
and with it corporate merch,

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.260
would continue to pull
from countercultures

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:36.800
to maintain their cool image.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:38.510
They'd especially take inspiration

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.350
from skateboarding, rap,

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.820
and internet communities,

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.180
grabbing font styles

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:44.840
and other design elements.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:45.780
In recent years,

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.640
brands like makeup company Glossier

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.040
would also start to
"borrow" streetwear brand

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.610
Supreme's marketing style.

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:53.480
They, too, manufacture scarcity

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.540
for popular merch

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.000
in order to generate hype.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.330
And over the past 10 years,

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.640
merch has become more important than ever,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.340
increasingly infiltrating fashion

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.780
and culture at large.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.081
This has been helped along, again,

00:09:04.081 --> 00:09:06.090
by increasingly streamlined

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:07.830
manufacturing and distribution,

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.640
as well as, you guessed it, the internet.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:11.473
And these days,

00:09:11.473 --> 00:09:12.420
you don't have to be Disney

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.770
for merch to be profitable.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:15.010
The rise of micromerch

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:16.210
catering to niche markets

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.190
now sustains everyone

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.130
from musicians to podcasters

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:20.490
to makeup influencers.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.650
Many creators now supplement their incomes

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.190
with genuinely cool merch,

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:25.660
some of which might even feature

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.770
a certain monocle-wearing jackass.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:28.800
During the pandemic,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.340
merch became especially vital

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.770
for those local bars,
restaurants, and bookstores

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:33.820
with enough cache to sell you

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.010
on a cute tank-top,

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:36.420
branded weed grinder,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.230
or a pair of earrings
shaped like a sandwich.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:40.550
The entangled phenomena

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.820
of the value-obsessed aspirational class,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:43.667
the value-laden brand,

00:09:43.667 --> 00:09:45.670
and of course, cheap manufacturing,

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.670
all converged to facilitate
the rise of merch.

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:50.517
As Amanda Mull writes in The Atlantic,

00:09:50.517 --> 00:09:52.497
"For decades, Americans have been trained

00:09:52.497 --> 00:09:54.087
"to see logos and slogans

00:09:54.087 --> 00:09:56.477
"as a primary means of self-expression

00:09:56.477 --> 00:09:59.130
"and even as a way of
telegraphing values."

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.300
And nothing does so
more clearly than merch.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.200
But what is merch really doing,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.370
on a deeper level?

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:06.690
To understand, let's return

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.350
to the aspirational class.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:09.220
The way we see it,

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:10.512
merch has become a new kind

00:10:10.512 --> 00:10:12.870
of conspicuous consumption.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:13.830
Rather than showing off

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:14.720
how wealthy you are,

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:16.810
though, it acts as a potent flex

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:19.360
about your cultural
savviness and your values.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:21.760
Merch is the natural
culmination of consumption

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:23.600
as an expression of identity.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.020
And if that consumption happens

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.610
to be just a little bit ironic,

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.880
a la the KFC crocs that
sold out in 30 minutes,

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:31.540
well, that's all the better.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:32.770
Some of this is the result

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:34.080
of successful marketing.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.130
Over the past decade,

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:36.770
as brand competition has increased

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.540
even more than it did in the 80s,

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.100
branding has increasingly shifted

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.170
to become more value-oriented.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.072
As Andjelic writes,

00:10:43.072 --> 00:10:45.087
"Brands shifted from increasing

00:10:45.087 --> 00:10:47.177
"value of their products through utility,

00:10:47.177 --> 00:10:50.150
"competitive comparison,
and creative advertising."

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:51.092
Instead, she explains,

00:10:51.092 --> 00:10:52.530
they focus on things like

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:55.037
sustainability, artisanship, and community

00:10:55.037 --> 00:10:56.297
"in order to give their products

00:10:56.297 --> 00:10:58.150
"identity and singularity."

00:10:58.150 --> 00:10:59.730
You're not wearing a Moon Juice shirt

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:00.710
because it fits better,

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:01.950
you're wearing it to show you value

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.670
wellness, clean living and crystals.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:06.670
Lighting up an A24 Horror candle

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:08.870
shows that you value independent cinema.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.050
Even if you, like me,

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:12.470
are too scared to actually
watch "Midsommar."

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:13.640
Their cultural potency

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:15.630
can render merch even more desirable

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.954
than the "real" brand products themselves.

00:11:17.954 --> 00:11:19.637
As Bluestein notes,

00:11:19.637 --> 00:11:20.997
"Whether or not they are actually

00:11:20.997 --> 00:11:22.387
"buying your core product,

00:11:22.387 --> 00:11:24.127
"merch with a point of view can resonate

00:11:24.127 --> 00:11:25.407
"with people who are somehow

00:11:25.407 --> 00:11:27.970
"finding it to be a form
of self-expression."

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:29.582
Merch, in representing a brand

00:11:29.582 --> 00:11:31.520
or artist's cultural cache,

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:34.010
confers that same status onto the buyer.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.820
People are telegraphing their values

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:37.506
and tastes by associating themselves

00:11:37.506 --> 00:11:39.930
with Jstor over Lexis Nexis

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.380
or with Cashapp over Venmo.

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:43.910
But to really understand

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:45.240
why a New Yorker tote bag

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:46.980
means so much to so many people,

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:50.150
let's return to what
brands as a whole mean.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:52.030
As media scholar Adam Arvidsson

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:53.127
writes in his book,

00:11:53.127 --> 00:11:55.617
"Brands: Meaning and
Value in Media Culture,"

00:11:55.617 --> 00:11:57.117
"Although brands have a long history

00:11:57.117 --> 00:11:58.697
"as a commercial institution,

00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:00.867
"reaching as far back as the 18th century,

00:12:00.867 --> 00:12:02.737
"their position as central components

00:12:02.737 --> 00:12:03.857
"of the social fabric

00:12:03.857 --> 00:12:06.270
"was established in the 1980s."

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.620
As we noted earlier,

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:08.910
this was around the time

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:09.800
when branding became

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:10.754
less about the product

00:12:10.754 --> 00:12:12.510
and more about the story

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:14.850
or "personality" of the brand itself.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.900
The result was powerful.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:18.557
As Arvidsson elaborates,

00:12:18.557 --> 00:12:20.037
"Brands now became something

00:12:20.037 --> 00:12:21.607
"of an omnipresent tool

00:12:21.607 --> 00:12:23.257
"by means of which identity,

00:12:23.257 --> 00:12:25.947
"social relations and shared experiences

00:12:25.947 --> 00:12:27.470
"could be constructed."

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:28.770
The web of modern brands

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:29.603
thus helps establish

00:12:29.603 --> 00:12:32.800
a "common social world"
that we all inhabit.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.530
This happened at the same time

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:35.830
as the late 20th century

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.200
collapse of community,

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.080
amidst widespread urbanization,

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.870
privatization of public spaces, and so on.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:43.456
So you maybe used to identify

00:12:43.456 --> 00:12:45.290
largely as a Catholic,

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.350
or a Dane, or a New Yorker.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:50.210
But the erosion of
religious, ethnic, cultural,

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:51.670
and other public communities

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.830
also erodes the significance
of that identity.

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:56.037
As Andjelic writes,

00:12:56.037 --> 00:12:56.927
"We are going through

00:12:56.927 --> 00:12:59.157
"the imagined community renaissance,

00:12:59.157 --> 00:13:01.047
"thanks to modern brands stepping in

00:13:01.047 --> 00:13:02.877
"as the social constructs of belonging

00:13:02.877 --> 00:13:05.330
"left vacant by traditional institutions."

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:06.377
She notes that, "Increasingly,

00:13:06.377 --> 00:13:09.480
"brands target communities
over customers."

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.150
Merch is an obvious way of telegraphing

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.170
your membership to a branded community,

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.600
whether via tote bag or koozie.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.380
And for the aspirational class,

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:19.420
distinguished not by furs and jewels

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.610
but by their taste,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:21.640
belonging to the right

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:23.900
branded communities is paramount.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:25.210
Lighting a dinnertime candle

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.320
in Victorian England meant

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.670
you had access to something

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:28.960
rare and expensive.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:32.060
Today, sprinkling
responsibly-sourced spices

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.730
on your dinner isn't
about conferring wealth,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:36.330
which few of us have anyway.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:39.150
It's more about living
your taste and values.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.550
It's not about "prestige and exclusivity"

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:43.590
but about "identity and belonging"

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.370
to certain taste communities.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:48.170
And because humans are
very literal creatures,

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:49.120
we've fixated on

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.110
the most literal way to do this.

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:52.430
Buy merch that spells out

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:54.440
your taste for everyone to see.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:56.930
And this isn't inherently sinister.

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:57.763
Here at Wisecrack,

00:13:57.763 --> 00:13:59.100
we love good merch.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.060
In fact, each of us owns
an estimated average

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:03.370
of 1.75 Grateful Dead T-shirts,

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.163
although I kind of skew the curve on that.

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.920
But this one is like colored,

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.130
and this one is like a whiter thing.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:13.240
This one has a totally different font

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.100
and it's from a different tour,

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.200
and this one was limited edition.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:19.770
Okay, they're all the same.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:20.843
But we think it's interesting

00:14:20.843 --> 00:14:23.370
that as young peoples'
economic opportunities

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:25.380
become less and less plentiful,

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.550
our consumption habits

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.900
are becoming increasingly

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.630
important to our identities.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:30.797
As Andjelic notes,

00:14:30.797 --> 00:14:32.477
"Unable to succeed economically,

00:14:32.477 --> 00:14:34.207
"millennials are turning their attention

00:14:34.207 --> 00:14:37.257
"to everyday things with
an almost obsessive,

00:14:37.257 --> 00:14:38.700
"laser-like focus."

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.540
It's why really liking coffee

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.550
or doing the right type of yoga

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:43.930
can almost constitute

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:46.320
an entire personality these days.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.030
But, what if our obsession with taste,

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:49.560
and thus, with cool merch,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.010
really has much more to do with

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:52.830
what we can't afford?

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.060
You know, the little things

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.500
like houses, cars, advanced degrees,

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.080
and quality medical care.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:00.170
Unable to achieve the markers of success

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:02.300
that our parents and
grandparents aspired to,

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.480
we've found other ways

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:04.490
to make our lives feel

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:05.930
richer and more meaningful.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:07.653
And more power to us for that.

00:15:07.653 --> 00:15:09.710
But merch has emerged as an easy

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:11.380
and relatively inexpensive way

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:12.930
to accrue social capital.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.630
Especially in a world where most of us

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:15.463
might never have,

00:15:15.463 --> 00:15:18.100
you know, capital-capital.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:19.190
It's enough to make you want

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:20.500
to take a nice long hit

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:21.630
from your limited-edition

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:23.490
Seth-Rogen-sculpted pipe.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:24.653
But what do you guys think?

00:15:24.653 --> 00:15:26.043
Is the popularity of merch

00:15:26.043 --> 00:15:27.690
a sinister indicator of

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:29.271
how much is wrong with our economy?

00:15:29.271 --> 00:15:30.610
Or is it just a fun way

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:31.870
to signal that you, too,

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:33.241
enjoy eating Oreos?

00:15:33.241 --> 00:15:34.835
Let us know in the comments.

00:15:34.835 --> 00:15:36.270
Big thanks to our patrons

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:37.210
for aligning yourselves

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:38.600
with the Wisecrack brand.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.910
And be sure to check out our podcasts.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.060
Hit subscribe like

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:43.070
it's the checkout button

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:44.470
on a Supreme drop day,

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.320
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.989
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:15:47.989 --> 00:15:48.944
Later.

00:15:48.944 --> 00:15:51.527
(upbeat music)

